<h2>Understanding License Types (Don't Skip This)</h2>

<table>
<tr><th>License Type</th><th>What It Means</th><th>Safe for YouTube?</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Royalty-Free</strong></td><td>Pay once (or use free), use forever. No per-use fees.</td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Creative Commons CC0</strong></td><td>Public domain. No restrictions, no credit needed.</td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Creative Commons CC BY</strong></td><td>Free to use, but you MUST credit the creator.</td><td>✅ Yes (with credit)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Creative Commons CC BY-NC</strong></td><td>Free for non-commercial use only. Monetized YouTube = commercial.</td><td>❌ Not for monetized channels</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Standard Copyright</strong></td><td>"All rights reserved." You cannot use without explicit permission.</td><td>❌ No</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="callout">
<strong>⚠️ The CC BY-NC trap:</strong> Many creators assume "Creative Commons" means "free for everything." Wrong. CC BY-NC (Non-Commercial) means you CANNOT use it on monetized YouTube videos, sponsored content, or client work. Always check for the "NC" restriction. I've seen editors get copyright claims because they didn't read the full license code.
</div>

<h2>FAQ</h2>

<details><summary>Can I use Bollywood/Indian film songs in YouTube videos?</summary>
<p>Short answer: no, not legally. Indian film music is copyrighted by labels like T-Series, Zee Music, Tips, etc. Using it without a license will result in a Content ID claim (your video's revenue goes to the label) or a takedown. If you want to use Bollywood music, you need a sync license — which costs lakhs for commercial use. Use royalty-free alternatives instead.</p>
</details>

<details><summary>Will YouTube's Content ID catch "free" music?</summary>
<p>Sometimes, yes. Some "free" music gets uploaded to Content ID by distributors who shouldn't have claimed it, or by the original artist who later changed their licensing terms. Always keep your download receipt/license confirmation. If you get a false claim, you can dispute it with proof of your license.</p>
</details>

<details><summary>Is ₹1,000/month for Epidemic Sound worth it as a beginner?</summary>
<p>If you're making even ₹5,000/month from editing — yes, absolutely. The time you save not searching for free tracks, plus the copyright safety, plus the consistently high quality makes it the best investment in your workflow. If you're not earning yet, use YouTube Audio Library (free) until you get your first paying clients.</p>
</details>

<details><summary>Can I use AI-generated music (Suno, Udio) in client work?</summary>
<p>Risky. Copyright law hasn't caught up with AI-generated music. Nobody has definitively ruled whether AI-generated content is copyrightable or who owns it. For personal/experimental projects, go ahead. For client work and monetized content, I'd stick with licensed royalty-free music from established libraries. The legal certainty is worth the cost.</p>
</details>

